{{Politics of Mongolia}}

Presidential elections were held in [[Mongolia]] on 22 May 2005. The result was a victory for [[Nambaryn Enkhbayar]] of the [[Mongolian People's Party|Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party]] (MPRP), who received over half of the vote in Mongolia's first four-way presidential election.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Former ruler wins Mongolia poll|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2005/5/23/former-ruler-wins-mongolia-poll|website=[[Al Jazeera]]|access-date=2026-01-31|language=en|date=2005-05-23|last=|first=}}</ref> [[Mendsaikhany Enkhsaikhan]] from the country's main opposition [[Democratic Party (Mongolia)|Democratic Party]] placed second with around 20% of the popular vote, while [[Bazarsadyn Jargalsaikhan]] of the [[Republican Party (Mongolia)|Republican Party]] and [[Badarchiin Erdenebat]] of the [[Motherland Party (Mongolia)|Motherland Party]] came in third and fourth, respectively.<ref>{{Cite journal|title=Mongolia in 2005: Sharing Power, Dealing with Corruption|url=https://online.ucpress.edu/as/article/46/1/79/3586/Mongolia-in-2005-Sharing-Power-Dealing-with|journal=Asian Survey|date=2006-02-01|issn=0004-4687|pages=79–84|volume=46|issue=1|doi=10.1525/as.2006.46.1.79|language=en|first=Nyamosor|last=Tuya|url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Mongolian Presidency 2005 General|url=https://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/1969/|website=[[IFES]]|access-date=2026-01-31|language=en}}</ref>

In the aftermath of his election as the 3rd [[President of Mongolia]], Nambaryn Enkhbayar became the first person in Mongolia's modern history to hold the offices of president, [[Prime Minister of Mongolia|prime minister]], and [[Chairman of the State Great Khural|speaker of parliament]].

==Background==
In the [[1997 Mongolian presidential election|1997 presidential election]], the chairman of the Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party (MPRP), [[Natsagiin Bagabandi]], was elected [[President of Mongolia|president]]. He was re-elected in 2001, but [[term limit]]s meant he could not stand again in 2005.<ref name=tracker>{{Cite web | url = http://www.angus-reid.com/tracker/view/30347/mongolia_2008 | title = Mongolia | accessdate = 1 March 2009 | publisher = [[Angus Reid Global Monitor]] | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20080512153143/http://www.angus-reid.com/tracker/view/30347/mongolia_2008 | archive-date = 12 May 2008 | url-status = usurped | df = dmy-all }}</ref>

In the aftermath of the [[2004 Mongolian legislative election|2004 parliamentary elections]], a [[hung parliament]] between the ruling MPRP and the opposition [[Motherland Democratic Coalition]] (MDC) was convened in July 2004. The MDC consisted of the [[Democratic Party (Mongolia)|Democratic Party]], the [[Motherland Party (Mongolia)|Motherland Party]] , and the [[Civil Will–Republican Party]]. The two parliamentary blocs, both without clear majorities, were forced to form a [[coalition government]] headed by [[Tsakhiagiin Elbegdorj]] in August.<ref name="communists">{{cite news| url = http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/asia-pacific/4570067.stm | title = Ex-communists win Mongolia race | accessdate = 1 March 2009 | date = 23 May 2005 | work = [[BBC Online]]}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Mongolia (07/07)|url=https://2009-2017.state.gov/outofdate/bgn/mongolia/89768.htm#:~:text=June%2027,%202004,four%20smaller%20parties.|access-date=2026-01-31|website=[[U.S. Department of State]]|date=July 2007}}</ref>

The MDC was dissolved in December 2004, following internal disagreements and disputes over nominations for the 2005 presidential election.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Эх орон-Ардчилал эвсэл задарлаа (2004.12.28)|url=https://forum.mn/post/78813|website=forum.mn|access-date=2026-01-31|first=|last=|date=2004-12-28|language=mn}}</ref>

==Candidates==
According to the [[Constitution of Mongolia|1992 Constitution of Mongolia]], only political parties with parliamentary representation can nominate a candidate for presidential elections.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Final monitoring report 2005 presidential election|url=https://forum.mn/uploads/site/1126/res_mat/Final_monitoring_reportEBurnee.pdf|access-date=2026-01-31|website=forum.mn|date=2005|publisher=Global International|language=en|page=4}}</ref> Prior to the 2005 election, there were five political parties represented in the State Great Khural. Out of the five parties, only the [[Civil Will–Republican Party]] didn't nominate a candidate.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|url=https://forum.mn/uploads/site/1126/res_mat/Final_monitoring_reportEBurnee.pdf|title=Final monitoring report 2005 presidential election|access-date=2026-01-31|website=forum.mn|publisher=Global International|page=|language=en|date=2005|pages=6–7}}</ref>

Candidate nominations opened on 22 March 2005. Ultimately, the [[General Election Commission of Mongolia|General Election Commission]] (GEC) registered four candidates for the 2005 presidential election.<ref name=":1" /> The MPRP candidate was former [[Prime Minister of Mongolia|Prime Minister]] and incumbent [[Chairman of the State Great Khural]] [[Nambaryn Enkhbayar]].<ref name="tracker" /> Enkhbayar had been [[Chairman of the Mongolian People's Party|chairman of the MPRP]] since 1997 and was regarded as the clear favourite in the election.<ref name="communists" /><ref name="inaugurates">{{Cite web| url = http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/2005-06/25/content_3132872.htm | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20060228034203/http://news.xinhuanet.com/english/2005-06/25/content_3132872.htm | url-status = dead | archive-date = 28 February 2006 | title = Mongolia inaugurates new president | accessdate = 1 March 2009 | date = 25 June 2005 | publisher = [[Xinhua News Agency]]}}</ref> Prior to his nomination by the MPRP, Enkhbayar was widely covered by the [[Mass media in Mongolia|Mongolian media]], with a negative tone from various citizen movements dominating the coverage.<ref name=":1" /> He ran on a platform to increase foreign investment and continue liberalising the [[Economy of Mongolia|economy]] to address [[poverty in Mongolia]].<ref name="prime">{{cite news| url = http://www.iht.com/articles/2005/05/23/news/mongol.php | title = Mongolia elects ex-prime minister | accessdate = 1 March 2009 | date = 24 May 2005 | newspaper = [[International Herald Tribune]]}}</ref><ref name="former">{{Cite web| url = https://www.voanews.com/a/a-13-2005-05-23-voa6-66383112/547897.html| title = Candidate of Former Communist Ruling Party Wins Mongolia Election | accessdate = 1 March 2009 | date = 23 May 2005 | publisher = [[Voice of America]]}}</ref>

Enkhbayar's main rival was former prime minister [[Mendsaikhany Enkhsaikhan]] of the [[Democratic Party (Mongolia)|Democratic Party]] (DP).<ref name="prime" /> Enkhsaikhan received support from anti-communists and called for lower taxes for businesses and subsidies for poorer families. However, he was disadvantaged by divisions within the DP<ref name="votes">{{cite news| url = http://www.iht.com/articles/2005/05/22/asia/web.0522mongolia.php | title = Mongolia votes for new president | accessdate = 1 March 2009 | date = 22 May 2005 | newspaper = [[International Herald Tribune]]}}</ref> after securing nomination in the party's primaries.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |last= |date=2008-11-10 |title=Дөрөв дэх Ерөнхийлөгчөө угтах Монголын улс төр |url=https://gogo.mn/r/45146 |access-date=2026-02-18 |website=gogo.mn |language=mn}}</ref>

The other two candidates were [[Bazarsad Jargalsaikhan|Bazarsadyn Jargalsaikhan]] of the [[Mongolian Republican Party]] and [[Badarchiin Erdenebat]] of the [[Motherland Party (Mongolia)|Motherland Party]] (MLP).<ref name=":3" /> Jargalsaikhan was one of the richest people in Mongolia and said that he could put his business skills to use as president. Erdenebat called for a referendum to expand the powers of the President.<ref name="votes" /> The [[Mongolian Traditional United Party]], an [[Extra-parliamentary opposition|extra-parliamentary]] party, expressed its support for Erdenebat and the MLP.<ref name=":1" />
