==Campaign==
About a million of Mongolia's population were eligible to vote in the election, with 3,800 [[polling station]]s across Mongolia. If no candidate secured over half of the votes in the first round of the election, then a [[Two-round system|run-off]] would be held on 5 June between the top two candidates. The campaign for the first round officially began on 7 April 2005.<ref name="votes" /><ref name="stability">{{cite news|url=http://www.signonsandiego.com/news/world/20050407-0109-mongolia.html|title=Mongolia eyes stability as presidential race begins|accessdate=1 March 2009|date=7 April 2005|newspaper=[[The San Diego Union-Tribune]]}}</ref>

A few days before the election on 12 May 2005, the DP, the RP, and the MLP called on the [[General Election Commission of Mongolia|General Election Commission]] (GEC) to resign, alleging that the election was not being run cleanly. They claimed the GEC had printed 80,000 extra ballots, which is 11% more than the allowed reserve, in order to cause confusion. The next day, the parties held a public meeting, "Fair Elections!" in [[Sükhbaatar Square]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|url=https://forum.mn/uploads/site/1126/res_mat/Final_monitoring_reportEBurnee.pdf|title=Final monitoring report 2005 presidential election|date=2005|access-date=2026-01-31|website=forum.mn|publisher=Global International|page=20|language=en}}</ref> The outgoing President said that the GEC should do everything possible to ensure the election was fair.<ref name="fair">{{cite news|url=http://english.people.com.cn/200505/19/eng20050519_185744.html|title=Mongolian president urges fair presidential election|accessdate=1 March 2009|date=19 May 2005|newspaper=[[People's Daily]]}}</ref> The GEC issued a public statement on 6 April, denying that a single party was over-represented. The commission stated that of the 282 persons working in the sub-regions, 94 were MPRP members, 63 DP, 30 MLP, and 17 RP. Out of the 5,296 persons working in the electoral districts, 5,296 were MPRP members, 4,331 DP, 949 MLP, and 286 RP.<ref name=":2" />

Before the election, there were protests by various citizens' movements in the capital, [[Ulaanbaatar]], calling for a more open electoral system and protesting against alleged corruption. However, international [[Election monitoring|election monitors]] reported that they did not find any irregularities in the election.<ref name="communists" />

Voting began at 7 am and [[voter turnout]] was high as usual in Mongolia. Many voters turned out in [[Culture of Mongolia#Clothing|traditional Mongolian attire]],<ref name="votes" /> some on horseback,<ref name="former" /> while elsewhere poll workers took [[ballot box]]es to nomadic Mongols who were unable to get to polling stations.<ref name="votes" />

== Opinion polls ==
Several opinion polls were conducted by the [[International Republican Institute]] (IRI), Sant Maral Foundation and other firms prior to the May 22 election. Enkhbayar and Enkhsaikhan were considered the main contenders in the race, with around 50% and 20% approval from the public, respectively.<ref name=":1" />
